## 15. AUTOMATIC COVERAGE OF ALL QUALIFYING FUTURE UPDATES, ADDITIONS, AND PROJECT MATERIALS

**15.1. Official branch.** For this Section, the Official Main Branch is exclusively the branch named exactly `main` in the Official Repository as defined in Section 2.1. No change to the hosting platform's default branch, no branch rename, and no copy or mirror changes this rule. A replacement branch qualifies only by an express written and signed amendment under Section 12.3.

**15.2. Automatic inclusion.** Subject to Sections 15.3 to 15.7, the Works automatically include every original research or project material created by the Licensor, or lawfully controlled by the Licensor with sufficient licensing authority, that is committed, uploaded, or merged into the Official Main Branch as part of the Research Collection. This includes all future updates, corrections, improvements, revisions, rewrites, adaptations, derivative versions, new releases, new projects, subprojects, folders, files, modules, scripts, source code, object code, binaries, algorithms, model architectures, model configurations, model weights, datasets, annotations, documentation, diagrams, tests, experimental materials, configuration files, build files, and other original project components, regardless of file format or whether separately identified in a README, tag, Release, or individual notice.

**15.3. Effective inclusion date.** Each qualifying item or change is included from the date and time of its first commit, upload, or merge into `main`, as recorded in the Official Repository's version-control history. Where a change is first committed to another branch, it is not included until it is merged or otherwise committed into `main`. A qualifying item does not need a separate release, tag, notice, or README reference. The inclusion rule applies prospectively from the item's inclusion date and does not retroactively alter the terms governing an earlier version or separately signed authorization.

**15.4. New projects and repository additions.** A new project, subproject, or component becomes part of the Research Collection when its original project or research materials are committed or merged into `main` as part of the repository's research or project content. No additional declaration by file, project, directory, release, or README is required. Mere presence on another branch, in a fork, in a mirror, or in an unrelated repository is insufficient.

**15.5. Sole-author rule and rights condition.** The Licensor intends future additions to the Research Collection to consist of original material created by him. Automatic inclusion defines the scope of the Works; it does not transfer ownership or create rights. If any future file or component is created or contributed in whole or in part by another person, it is not covered as Author-Owned Material unless the Licensor has first obtained from the actual rights holder all rights, permissions, assignments, and authorizations necessary to grant the permissions in this License, including any right to authorize recipients or sublicensees where required. Where rights may belong to an employer, university, research institution, funding body, client, commissioning party, or other organization, authorization must be obtained from the relevant rights holder or duly authorized entity. Submission, acceptance, merge, commit, storage, modification, or publication in `main` does not by itself transfer ownership or grant the Licensor a right to sublicense. The Licensor shall not represent another person's contribution as his own original authorship merely because it appears in `main`.

**15.6. Third-party and AI-related materials.** No Third-Party Material becomes Author-Owned Material by being committed to `main`, modified, combined, stored, or distributed alongside a Work. The inclusion of datasets, model weights, pretrained models, training data, validation data, test data, metadata, outputs, or AI-related components does not represent or presume that the Licensor owns or controls the underlying data, model, weights, works, contractual permissions, or third-party rights. Original code, configurations, and documentation may be covered separately only to the extent the Licensor has the necessary rights in those separable components. Applicable third-party license terms, attribution requirements, use restrictions, redistribution conditions, source-disclosure obligations, privacy duties, and other legal or contractual conditions remain applicable. Material must not be incorporated or distributed in a manner that violates those conditions. Where compatibility or permission is uncertain, the affected Third-Party Material must be excluded, replaced, or separately authorized before the relevant distribution.

**15.7. Removal, replacement, and prior distributions.** Removing, replacing, modifying, or relocating material in `main` does not, by itself, revoke permissions already validly granted under this License for versions lawfully distributed or licensed before the change, subject to the termination provisions of this License and applicable law. This clause does not validate an unauthorized distribution, create rights the Licensor did not possess, or cure a failure to obtain necessary rights. Later versions are included from their own inclusion dates.

**15.8. Traceability records.** The Licensor shall take reasonable steps, where information is available and lawful to retain, to preserve records of material provenance and licensing status. Records may include commit identifiers, dates, contributor identities, source references, written assignments or permissions, third-party license notices, and relevant provenance information for datasets, model weights, pretrained models, and external components. Such records support verification but do not themselves establish ownership, create a license, replace a required authorization, or cure a lack of rights.

**15.9. Relationship with the master-license rule.** This Section is part of the sole general License issued by the Licensor for Author-Owned Material in the Research Collection. It does not make any other project-level license file, README notice, release notice, archive, branch, fork, or mirror a competing general license. It does not invalidate third-party rights or licenses, rights validly granted before the Effective Date that remain legally effective, or mandatory legal rights. No new separate authorization is offered under this Section. The automatic inclusion mechanism determines the scope of the Works only; it is not an assignment of ownership, a presumption of authorship, or a substitute for the rights needed to grant the permissions in this License.

